{"subjectId":"data-science","id":"tokenization","title":"Tokenization, Stemming & Lemmatization","group":"Natural Language Processing","order":15,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Before a computer can work with text, it has to be cut into pieces (tokens) and words have to be brought to a common form.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">About this module</div><p>The notebook calls this module “Deep Learning”, but everything in it is <strong>text processing (NLP)</strong> — the groundwork that comes before any language model.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-question-mark\"></i>Why not just split()?</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>txt = \"Hello Geeks. We're hoping you guys are doing great.\"\nprint(len(txt.split(\".\")))      # 3        ← counts an empty piece after the last \".\"\nprint(txt.split()[:3])          # ['Hello', 'Geeks.', \"We're\"]   ← punctuation stuck to words</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-scissors\"></i>Tokenizing with NLTK</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from nltk.tokenize import sent_tokenize, word_tokenize\n\ntxt = \"Hello Geeks. We're hoping you guys are doing great.\"\nprint(len(sent_tokenize(txt)))                    # 2\nprint(word_tokenize(txt))                         # ['Hello', 'Geeks', '.', 'We', \"'re\", 'hoping', 'you', 'guys', 'are', 'doing', 'great', '.']\nprint([w for w in word_tokenize(txt) if w.isalnum()])   # ['Hello', 'Geeks', 'We', 'hoping', 'you', 'guys', 'are', 'doing', 'great']</code></pre></figure>\n<figure class=\"code\"><figcaption>Setup</figcaption><pre><code>pip install nltk\npython -m nltk.downloader punkt punkt_tab stopwords wordnet omw-1.4</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>sent_tokenize</code> knows that “Dr.” or “U.K.” don't end a sentence; <code>word_tokenize</code> splits off punctuation and contractions (“We're” → “We”, “'re”).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-compare\"></i>Stemming vs lemmatization</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from nltk.stem import PorterStemmer, WordNetLemmatizer\nstem, lem = PorterStemmer(), WordNetLemmatizer()\n\nwords = [\"change\", \"changes\", \"changed\", \"studies\", \"better\"]\nprint([stem.stem(w) for w in words])              # ['chang', 'chang', 'chang', 'studi', 'better']\nprint([lem.lemmatize(w) for w in words])          # ['change', 'change', 'changed', 'study', 'better']\nprint(lem.lemmatize(\"changed\", pos=\"v\"), lem.lemmatize(\"better\", pos=\"a\"))   # change good</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Stemming</th><th>Lemmatization</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How</td><td>Chops suffixes by rules</td><td>Looks words up in a dictionary (WordNet)</td></tr><tr><td>Output</td><td>May not be a real word (<code>chang</code>)</td><td>Always a real word (<code>change</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Speed</td><td>Fast</td><td>Slower</td></tr><tr><td>Needs part of speech</td><td>No</td><td>For best results (<code>pos=\"v\"</code> verb, <code>\"a\"</code> adjective)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Without <code>pos</code>, the lemmatizer treats every word as a noun — that's why the notebook got <code>changed</code> back unchanged. Tell it the word is a verb and it returns <code>change</code>.</p></div></section>"}